{"kind":"request","text":"Make \"needs my action\" include ratification, because the one obligation only Hugh can discharge is the one it cannot show.\n\nHugh reported that two things he was told he must do do not appear in the panel. Investigating gave three separate causes, and the third is the one worth fixing here.\n\nOne of the two was never filed durably at all — my failure, now filed. The other, the charter request 0fe20fcc, is a genuine open commitment addressed to his fingerprint and should appear; if it does not, the likely cause is that `me` is undefined, because WorkDrawer.tsx resolves it as the fingerprint of the actor whose name equals `session.actor` and only when exactly one configured actor matches. A browser session with no actor, or a name matching none or two, empties the panel silently for everyone.\n\nThe structural gap is this. `workItemNeedsAction` switches on `commitment.status` and nothing else, so the panel can only ever show commitments. Ratification is not a commitment. Right now the live log holds 8 unratified proposals and 39 unratified asserts, and Hugh holds the only `ratifier` role in the room — so his principal duty is exactly the population the panel is blind to. He has been finding it by being told in conversation, which is not a queue.\n\nThere is a second, smaller undercount beside it: `needsCount` sums over items inside topics, so a commitment whose root is not a topic is invisible. That is the same shape as the defect in the work counts fixed at 804bcd1f, where two of three figures were summed over topics while the third came from the projection.\n\nWorth deciding rather than assuming: not every unratified act is owed to somebody. An assert usually stands on its author's signature and never needs ratification, so counting all 39 would be noise, and noise is how a signal stops being read. What is genuinely owed is narrower — kinds whose lifecycle requires ratification to take force, addressed to an actor holding the role that can give it. That distinction is the work.","body":{"conditions":"The personal work panel shows what an actor actually owes: commitments as now, and acts awaiting a ratification that actor is authorized to give, derived from the vocabulary's satisfier rules rather than from a hardcoded list of kinds, so an act that needs no ratification is never counted; the count is computed over the projection rather than summed over topics, so a commitment or act whose root is not a topic is not invisible; the panel distinguishes the two populations rather than adding them into one figure, following the separation established at 804bcd1f; when `me` cannot be resolved the panel says so rather than rendering an empty queue that reads as nothing owed; a test pins that an unratified act needing no ratification is not counted, that one needing it is, and that an item outside every topic still appears; `npx tsc --noEmit` and the UI tests pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}